  "repaired_windows": [],
  "recognised_text": "Imagine you're generating text using a massive language model. Normally, the model predicts one token, then another, then another. Every new token requires another forward pass. And when your model has billions of parameters, repeatedly loading those weights becomes expensive. But what if we could predict several tokens ahead and verify them together? That's the idea behind speculative decoding. And SpecInfer takes this a step further. First, we introduce a smaller, faster draft model. Instead of asking our expensive target model to generate every token, the draft model proposes several possible continuations. But rather than committing to just one sequence, SpecInfer organizes these predictions into a branching token tree. Each path represents a different possible continuation of the sentence. Now comes the interesting part. How can the large model verify all these different paths at once, without mixing their information? SpecInfer uses tree-based attention. We pack the candidate tokens into a single verification structure and construct a special attention mask. Each token can attend to the original prompt and its own ancestors in the tree, but it cannot see tokens from unrelated branches. This lets the target model evaluate multiple candidate continuations in parallel using a single forward pass. For greedy decoding, the verifier checks which proposed tokens agree with the target model's predictions. Matching tokens along the selected path are accepted. When a prediction disagrees, the target model supplies the correct next token, and the remaining speculative continuation is discarded. Then, the process repeats. Here's why this is powerful. Instead of spending an expensive target model pass generating just one token, we can potentially advance several tokens at once. And unlike simply trusting the smaller model, the verification process preserves the target model's intended output. Of course, larger trees aren't automatically better. More bredges create additional verification work. Poor draft predictions reduce the benefit. But when the draft model is accurate and verification is efficient, SPECInfer can significantly reduce generation latency. The key insight is simple. Don't make your largest model discover every token sequentially. Let smaller models speculate. Then let the large model verify many possibilities together. That's the intuition behind Specinfer.",
  "speech_wpm": 139.9
 },
